option.shadowDistance.comment=The shadow render distance controls how far away \
	terrain can potentially cast or receive real-time shadows.\n\n\
	Terrain outside of this distance will cast and receive approximate, static \
	shadows based on skylight.\n\n\
	Lower distances mean that less terrain will be rendered, improving frame \
	rates.\n\n\
	The actual shadow render distance is capped by the View Distance setting \
	in Video Settings..

option.SURFACE_COLORS=Surface colors
value.SURFACE_COLORS.STANDARD=Standard
value.SURFACE_COLORS.NONE=None (whiteworld)
value.SURFACE_COLORS.VERTEX_COLOR=Vertex color (foliage)
option.SURFACE_COLORS.comment=When None, the object's base color is \
	disregarded in favor of simply treating every surface as white. \
	With vertex color, only the texture color is disregarded, so foliage \
	remains green. \
	Useful for debugging lighting. \
	Alpha testing is still applied.

option.PBR_FORMAT=Material format
value.PBR_FORMAT.PBR_OFF=Off
value.PBR_FORMAT.LAB_PBR=LabPBR
option.PBR_FORMAT.comment=Whether to read the normal (_n) and specular (_s) \
	maps of a PBR resource pack, following the shaderLABS LabPBR 1.3 standard. \
	Each of the individual material features below can be switched off on top \
	of this.\n\n\
	It only affects the block atlas - terrain, and blocks held or placed. \
	Entities, held items, and Distant Horizons terrain are lit exactly as \
	before.\n\n\
	Set this to Off when playing without a PBR resource pack if you would \
	rather not pay for reading the material maps. Resource packs without \
	normal or specular maps decode to a flat normal, no reflection, and no \
	emission, so leaving it on is safe either way..

option.PBR_PARALLAX=Parallax mapping
option.PBR_PARALLAX.comment=Makes the height stored in the alpha channel of \
	the normal map displace the surface, so that deep parts of a texture appear \
	to sit further back than shallow ones and the surface reads as having real \
	depth.\n\n\
	This is the most expensive material feature. It traces the surface along \
	the view ray, one layer at a time, so its cost is set by the number of \
	layers below.\n\n\
	A held block is left out of the trace: its view ray and its block atlas \
	bounds are not the ones the trace is written against, and what came out was \
	a smear laid across the surface. It keeps its material, its normal map and \
	its height field self shadow - it simply has no parallax depth of its own.\n\n\
	It fades out with distance, and a resource pack with a flat height channel \
	is effectively free.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_STEPS=Parallax layers
option.PBR_PARALLAX_STEPS.comment=How many layers the parallax ray march \
	takes. Each layer is one texture sample for the view ray and, while Parallax \
	self-shadowing is on, one more for the light ray - so this sets the cost of \
	both marches. More layers track the height field more closely at shallow \
	viewing angles.\n\n\
	This is the option that decides how much of the height field's shape comes \
	out, and it is the only one that does. Parallax refinement can only narrow \
	down a crossing the layers already bracketed, so it cannot recover a surface \
	whose height varies more than once inside one of these layers: the size of \
	that gap is set here and nowhere else.\n\n\
	What it cannot do is take away the fine grain or banding on an ordinary block \
	face. More layers only cut that error finer and pack it closer together, so \
	it reads as a few bands at a low count and as grain at a high one - the same \
	error, shown differently. That error is the height channel being read without \
	interpolation, which is what Smooth parallax is for; what is left for this \
	option and for the refinement below is accuracy, not the removal of it.\n\n\
	Too few layers show up as flat shelf-like patches where the shape should be \
	- a crossing landing far from where it belongs on a surface that rises \
	sharply. Too many are simply wasted samples.\n\n\
	The default is 16, down from 32: with the height channel interpolated, this \
	only bounds the gap the refinement searches, and raising it moves the result \
	by less than a thousandth of a texel - so the half of the cost that was worth \
	paying back has been. With Smooth parallax off there is no such bill to pay, \
	as a height sample is one fetch again, and this can go straight back up. \
	Sundial defaults to 80 layers and Mellow to 64, but both of those describe a \
	march whose height samples are unfiltered; the top of this option's range is \
	still set where they are.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_REFINE=Parallax refinement
option.PBR_PARALLAX_REFINE.comment=How many binary search steps are used to \
	pin down where the view ray crosses the height field.\n\n\
	The layer march can only land on a layer boundary, and a height channel \
	usually covers only a fraction of its range, so few layers fall inside the \
	real variation. With too little refinement the texture visibly snaps \
	between a handful of positions instead of following the surface.\n\n\
	What it cannot do is recover shape the march did not bracket. It narrows \
	down a crossing, it does not look for a better one - so a surface whose \
	height varies more than once inside one pair of layers keeps whatever that \
	pair made of it however many steps it is given. Parallax layers is the \
	option that decides that, and the one to raise if the surface looks like it \
	is built out of flat sheets; this one is accuracy, and only accuracy. \
	Sundial's own settings menu draws the same line between its two knobs.\n\n\
	It is also the cheapest accuracy in this file: a bisection doubles its \
	accuracy per step, and 8 steps already land inside a four-thousandth of the \
	interval the layers handed it - far below one texel of any resource pack. \
	The old default of 32 was covering for a march short enough to need it, and \
	with the layer count raised it is no longer doing anything; raise it and the \
	samples are spent for nothing, lower it to save them.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SMOOTH=Smooth parallax
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SMOOTH.comment=Interpolates the height channel between its \
	texels, instead of reading it one texel at a time.\n\n\
	A block atlas is sampled one texel at a time as it is magnified - that is what \
	keeps a block's texture sharp - and a height channel drawn with a slope in it \
	therefore reads back as a staircase from one texel to the next. This option \
	fetches the four texels around each sample and mixes them by hand, which is \
	what a filter would do, so the field the parallax traces is the slope the \
	resource pack drew rather than a staircase.\n\n\
	The difference shows on height channels with hard edges - cobblestone, \
	bricks, anything built out of separate lumps. It turns their steps into \
	slopes. Sundial carries the same option and ships it on, and its own menu \
	describes it as making the parallax show a slope rather than individual \
	cubes.\n\n\
	Turn it off to keep the height field exactly as the resource pack drew it. It \
	is sharper that way, and the grain and banding come back wherever the field \
	has a hard edge - which is what this pack looked like before the option \
	existed. It is also the cheaper of the two settings: a height sample is one \
	fetch instead of four, so Parallax layers can go up and spend what this frees.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_DEPTH=Parallax depth
option.PBR_PARALLAX_DEPTH.comment=How deep the height field is at its deepest \
	point, as a fraction of the sprite the face is drawn with - 1.0 being the \
	whole width of the texture.\n\n\
	The displacement you actually see is this multiplied by the slope of the \
	view angle - 0 when looking straight at the surface, which is the geometry \
	of parallax mapping rather than the effect being switched off.\n\n\
	It is also capped by Maximum displacement, and stops changing once it goes \
	past it: raise both together when you want more depth.\n\n\
	This used to be a distance in blocks, converted into texture coordinates \
	through the surface's own UV axes. On a face whose texture is stretched or \
	squashed in the world - a door, a thin face of a model, a texture drawn \
	across several blocks - those axes come out skewed with it, and the depth \
	they produced was wildly too large or too small. That is what left whole \
	faces with no parallax at all while this setting did nothing on them. \
	Measured against the sprite instead, the same number means the same width \
	of texture on every face.\n\n\
	On an ordinary block, where one sprite covers one face, the two readings \
	are the same, so the default of 0.2 is the fifth of a block it has always \
	been.\n\n\
	LabPBR asks resource packs to keep this within a quarter of a block, so \
	values much above 0.25 will likely look wrong on most packs.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_MAX_OFFSET=Maximum displacement
option.PBR_PARALLAX_MAX_OFFSET.comment=How far a sample is allowed to be \
	displaced, in the same units as Parallax depth: a fraction of one sprite. \
	It is what keeps a glancing view angle from exaggerating the depth, since \
	the displacement is divided by the slope of that angle.\n\n\
	Keep it at least as large as Parallax depth. Below that, turning the depth \
	up stops doing anything once it passes this value - which is what the old \
	default of 0.25 did to a depth setting that goes up to 0.5. This is why it \
	defaults to 0.5.\n\n\
	Past half a sprite a sample has left the material it came from, and it is \
	not allowed to read there at all. A sample that would leave the sprite has \
	its offset faded out by the room the fragment has left, and anything that \
	still reaches the boundary is held at the sprite's edge. Neither wrapping \
	round to the other side of the sprite nor freezing a strip at its edge is \
	allowed to happen, and that is this pack's own rule, set on 2026-09-22 - it \
	is where this parts company with Mellow and Sundial, both of which wrap, and \
	what wrapping costs is that a ray reads material belonging to the far side of \
	the face, which is how a door's window was being seen through the frame \
	around it.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_DISTANCE=Parallax distance
option.PBR_PARALLAX_DISTANCE.comment=Distance in meters at which parallax \
	mapping fades out, then stops entirely.\n\n\
	Past a certain distance a single screen pixel covers more than the whole \
	height range, so the ray march costs full price and produces nothing but \
	shimmer.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW=Parallax self-shadowing
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW.comment=Whether the height field should cast shadows \
	on itself.\n\n\
	Parallax mapping on its own only slides the texture around: the raised parts \
	do not darken what is behind them, so the surface reads as a flat picture \
	pasted onto the block. Shadowing the height field against the light is what \
	makes the sides of bumps look like sides, and is the single biggest \
	improvement to how deep the effect looks.\n\n\
	Costs one round of texture samples per pixel, and is skipped entirely for \
	materials with a flat height channel.

option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW_STRENGTH=Shadow strength
option.PBR_PARALLAX_SHADOW_STRENGTH.comment=How dark the height field shadows \
	get. 1.0 is a fully occluded crevice.\n\n\
	Too high can look like dirt rather than shadow on resource packs with a \
	very bumpy height channel.

option.PBR_TANGENT_ATTRIBUTE=Geometry tangents
option.PBR_TANGENT_ATTRIBUTE.comment=Whether to build the material tangent frame \
	from the tangent the geometry actually has, instead of rebuilding one out of \
	screen-space derivatives.\n\n\
	The derivative route has to reconstruct the surface position from the depth \
	buffer before it can differentiate it, and the depth buffer quantizes: past a \
	certain distance the step between two neighbouring pixels is larger than the \
	distance between those pixels across the surface, so the frame stops \
	describing the surface and the normal map bends the normal along axes that \
	have nothing to do with the material.\n\n\
	The symptom is a normal map that appears to invert from a fixed distance \
	outwards, and it cannot be tuned away with the mip or parallax settings, \
	because neither of those is what is wrong - the distance depends on the depth \
	buffer's precision and on how obliquely the surface is seen, not on the \
	resource pack.\n\n\
	The vertex tangent has none of that to worry about: it comes from the quad \
	itself, and interpolating it across a flat triangle is exact. This is on by \
	default, since it is a correctness fix rather than a look.\n\n\
	Turn it off to compare the two on the same scene. Geometry that carries no \
	tangent at all falls back to the old method automatically, so a mob or a \
	modded model cannot break because of this.

option.PBR_NORMAL_MAP=Normal mapping
option.PBR_NORMAL_MAP.comment=Whether the normal map should bend the surface \
	normal, which is what makes texture detail react to the direction of the \
	light.\n\n\
	Turning this off leaves the height channel of the same texture available to \
	parallax mapping, so the two can be used independently.

option.PBR_NORMAL_STRENGTH=Normal strength
option.PBR_NORMAL_STRENGTH.comment=How far the normal map is allowed to bend \
	the surface normal.\n\n\
	Raise this if the surface detail is there but too subtle to notice, or lower \
	it if the lighting starts to look noisy or dirty.

option.PBR_MATERIAL_MAX_LOD=Material detail limit
option.PBR_MATERIAL_MAX_LOD.comment=The finest level of the material maps that \
	the block atlas is allowed to be sampled at. The default of 0 samples the \
	full-resolution texture everywhere.\n\n\
	The block atlas has no useful data past the edge of a sprite, and an empty \
	texel is black - which is not a neutral value for a normal map. Once a level \
	of detail is coarse enough for its texels to reach into that empty space, \
	every sample near a sprite's edge picks up that black, and the surface's \
	normal map appears to tilt or invert. Since the level of detail grows with \
	distance, this appears as a hard line across the ground a fixed distance \
	from the player, and a sharper resource pack reaches it sooner.\n\n\
	Raising this restores filtering on distant material maps, at the risk of \
	that artefact coming back; PBR_NORMAL_FADE_LOD below is what keeps it away \
	when you do. Set this to the Material detail limit debug view first if you \
	want to see which level a given distance actually lands on.

option.PBR_NORMAL_FADE_LOD=Normal fade distance
option.PBR_NORMAL_FADE_LOD.comment=The level of detail at which normal mapping \
	has faded out to a flat surface entirely, reached gradually from about 40% \
	of this value.\n\n\
	A normal map finer than a screen pixel cannot be resolved, so all it does at \
	a distance is shimmer. Fading it out leaves the material's colour and \
	reflection intact while removing the noise.\n\n\
	Lower this if distant terrain looks noisy; raise it if you would rather keep \
	the surface detail and accept some sparkle.


option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO=Material occlusion
option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO.comment=Whether to darken the ambient and block light that \
	reaches a fragment by the occlusion the resource pack baked into the blue \
	channel of the normal map.\n\n\
	That channel is the third of the three things LabPBR stores in a normal map: \
	X and Y are the normal, alpha is the height, and blue is occlusion. It is \
	baked from the block's own geometry - the inside of a brick, the corners of a \
	plank, the gaps between leaves - all of which Minecraft's per-vertex ambient \
	occlusion cannot produce, since it only knows about block corners.\n\n\
	Off by default, as it is the one material feature that changes how bright \
	familiar blocks are: a pack with strong occlusion makes the world read darker \
	than Steadfast's original rendering. Before switching it on, look at a block \
	with the Occlusion channel selected under the material debug view to see what \
	the pack actually authored.\n\n\
	Only ambient and block light are affected. Sunlight is left to the shadow map \
	and to parallax self-shadowing, so a sunlit surface is never darkened twice.

option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO_STRENGTH=Occlusion strength
option.PBR_MATERIAL_AO_STRENGTH.comment=How much of that occlusion is applied. \
	1.0 uses the channel exactly as the resource pack authored it; lower it if \
	the pack overdoes the effect.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Material occlusion is off.

option.PBR_SPECULAR=Specular reflections
option.PBR_SPECULAR.comment=Whether the smoothness and reflectance (F0) stored \
	in the specular map should drive a specular highlight.\n\n\
	Rough surfaces get a broad, soft sheen and smooth ones a sharp highlight, \
	which is what separates polished stone from plastic.

option.PBR_SPECULAR_STRENGTH=Specular strength
option.PBR_SPECULAR_STRENGTH.comment=How strong the specular reflection is.\n\n\
	1.0 is the physically correct value, but a correct dielectric reflects only \
	about 4% of the light that hits it, and Steadfast tonemaps its highlights \
	down on top of that, so the correct value is much less visible than most \
	people expect from a reflective material.\n\n\
	Raise this if surfaces still look flat; lower it towards 1.0 if everything \
	looks wet or plasticky.\n\n\
	With Energy conservation on, 1.0 is the value that keeps the light budget \
	balanced and anything above it is deliberate over-reflection.

option.PBR_LIGHT_SIZE=Sun and moon size
option.PBR_LIGHT_SIZE.comment=How big the sun and the moon are, as far as the \
	highlight they make on a surface is concerned.\n\n\
	Both are treated as points, which is why a polished block shows a highlight \
	the size of a single pixel instead of the disc a mirror really shows. The sun \
	covers about half a degree of sky, and this is that angle: it widens the \
	highlight to the sun's real size.\n\n\
	1.0 is the sun's actual angular size. 0.0 is the point light this used to be, \
	which is the setting to compare against; larger values exaggerate the disc \
	for the look of it, the way a photographer's starburst does.\n\n\
	What this does is make a mirror a little less perfect: a surface smooth \
	enough to show a highlight smaller than the sun now shows the sun's disc. A \
	rough surface's highlight is already far wider than the sun, so it gains only \
	a small amount - a few percent at ordinary roughness. The sun and moon drawn \
	in the sky have a disc of their own and do not change with this.

option.PBR_ENERGY_CONSERVATION=Energy conservation
option.PBR_ENERGY_CONSERVATION.comment=Whether to weight the diffuse light by \
	the part of it that the surface did not reflect, so that diffuse and \
	specular cannot add up to more light than arrived.\n\n\
	Fresnel decides the split: a surface reflects a fraction of the light hitting \
	it and lets the rest through into the material, and that transmitted part is \
	what the diffuse response is made of. Adding the two together without it is \
	what lets a shiny surface be brighter than the light falling on it, and what \
	makes a metal - which reflects most of the light and should have almost no \
	diffuse response - still take half a diffuse response and read as shiny \
	plastic instead.\n\n\
	Off by default, as it changes the brightness of everything with a specular \
	map at once. Worth pairing with a specular strength of 1.0, which is the \
	value at which the budget balances; 2.0 was only ever a way of fighting the \
	tonemapper.\n\n\
	Note that bright metals become noticeably darker in caves and at night, \
	which is correct but is a large enough change in look to be worth trying \
	rather than assuming.\n\n\
	Sky reflections are the natural companion to this: they are what a metal \
	reflects once its diffuse response is gone, which is what keeps it looking \
	like a metal rather than like a hole in the world.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS=Sky reflections
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether a material reflects the sky, with a \
	direction and a roughness, instead of reflecting a flat wash of the light \
	around it.\n\n\
	Without this, the only environment a material reflects is the ambient light \
	at that point, which has no direction: a polished block reflects the same \
	thing whichever way it faces, and a rough surface is exactly as reflective as \
	a mirror. With it on, the colour comes from the sky model along the reflected \
	ray, so a surface facing the sun picks up the bright part of the sky, one \
	facing away picks up the darker part, and a rough surface has its ray bent \
	towards its own normal until it reflects the sky above it rather than a \
	mirror image.\n\n\
	On its own it reflects the sky and not the world. Screen-space reflections \
	below is what adds the world; this option is the part that always applies, \
	and it is what a ray that finds nothing falls back to.\n\n\
	One sky model evaluation per covered pixel, which makes it the most expensive \
	option here. Turn it off if the frame budget is tight.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_STRENGTH=Sky reflection strength
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_STRENGTH.comment=How strong the sky reflection is. 1.0 is \
	the value it was tuned at.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Sky reflections is off, and no effect indoors or \
	underground either, where there is no sky to reflect.

option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_ROUGHNESS=Roughness spread
option.PBR_REFLECTIONS_ROUGHNESS.comment=How far a rough surface bends the \
	reflected ray towards its own normal.\n\n\
	At 1.0 a fully rough surface looks straight up, which is the average of the \
	sky above it. At 0.0 roughness would not affect the direction at all, and a \
	rough surface would show the same mirror image a polished one does.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Sky reflections is off.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_SKY_MIN=Sky needed to reflect
option.PBR_REFLECTION_SKY_MIN.comment=How much sky a surface has to see before it \
	reflects any of it.\n\n\
	Minecraft's sky light spreads sideways under an overhang and passes straight \
	through glass, so a block can be lit as if it were outdoors while nothing of \
	the sky is visible from it - which is what a polished block on the floor of a \
	shallow cave reflecting a noon sky looks like. Keeping this near the top of \
	the range is what stops that.\n\n\
	The one case it cannot fix is a surface lit through a glass window: its sky \
	light is 15, exactly like one standing in the open, and nothing available here \
	can tell the two apart. Lower this if you would rather have reflections \
	indoors and put up with them in caves.\n\n\
	Only the sky part of the reflection is affected by it. What a traced ray finds \
	is already lit for where it is, so a metal in a cave reflecting the cave \
	around it stays.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_SMOOTHNESS_MIN=Smoothness needed to reflect
option.PBR_REFLECTION_SMOOTHNESS_MIN.comment=How smooth a surface has to be before \
	it reflects the sky at all.\n\n\
	Below this it reflects nothing whatever. The reflectance on its own is not a \
	gate: it falls off as a surface gets rougher but never reaches zero, and the \
	ground is always seen at a glancing angle, where the Fresnel term rises \
	towards 1.0 whatever the surface is. The two together put a wash of sky colour \
	over stone and dirt and made a whole landscape look faintly frosted.\n\n\
	At the default, anything rougher than about 0.25 in this pack's roughness \
	reflects nothing, and the reflection comes in above that as the square root of \
	how far past the threshold the surface is. This is the curve Sundial uses for \
	the same job.\n\n\
	Raise it to restrict reflections to shinier blocks, lower it to let them onto \
	duller ones. 0.0 disables the gate.\n\n\
	It scales the traced reflection along with the sky: a surface too rough to \
	reflect the sky has no business showing a mirror image of the world either.

option.PBR_METAL_REFLECTION_STRENGTH=Metal reflection strength
option.PBR_METAL_REFLECTION_STRENGTH.comment=How strong a metal's environment \
	reflection is, separately from the strength everything else uses.\n\n\
	A metal gets its own because the general one is not describing metals at all: \
	it holds back a wash of environment that used to land on every surface, and a \
	metal is the case where the reflection is the material rather than a sheen \
	over it. Held back, a metal does not read as restrained, it reads as dull. \
	The reference packs give metals no such factor, so 1.0 is the value that \
	matches them.\n\n\
	Turn it down if metals are too bright in a scene; that is a judgement about \
	the scene rather than about the physics.

option.PBR_REFLECTION_BLUR=Reflection blur
option.PBR_REFLECTION_BLUR.comment=How far the reflection of the world is spread \
	out at a roughness of 1.0, in pixels.\n\n\
	A rough surface does not reflect what a mirror reflects: a whole cone of \
	directions arrives at the same pixel, and one sharp sample of that cone shows \
	the mirror image the surface should not have. Spreading the sample over the \
	cone is what makes a brushed or frosted metal read as brushed or frosted \
	rather than as a mirror with its colours turned down. At 0.0 the reflection is \
	as sharp as it is on a surface with no material data at all.\n\n\
	The radius follows roughness and not distance, which is worth knowing before \
	wondering why a distant reflection is not blurrier: a cone of a given angle \
	covers about the same number of pixels whatever it lands on.\n\n\
	Only surfaces the reflection gate lets through pay for this, which is a small \
	part of any scene; each of those pays eight samples instead of one.

option.PBR_SSR=Screen-space reflections
option.PBR_SSR.comment=Whether a reflection may include the world and not only \
	the sky, by tracing the reflected ray through the depth buffer.\n\n\
	Sky reflections on their own make everything with a reflectance pick up the \
	same wash of sky, which reads as a frosted sheen rather than as a reflection. \
	With this on, a polished block reflects the terrain, the trees, and whatever \
	else the ray finds on screen.\n\n\
	It can only reflect what is on screen, and only from the previous frame: a \
	shader mod cannot see what is behind you, and the frame being drawn is not \
	finished at the point the reflection is worked out. A ray that finds nothing \
	falls back to the sky, so the result degrades towards the sky-only reflection \
	rather than to nothing at all.\n\n\
	Very expensive: the trace runs on every covered pixel that passes the \
	roughness limit, and the two settings below it are what decide how much it \
	costs and which surfaces it applies to. Turn it off if the frame budget is \
	tight.

option.PBR_SSR_STEPS=Reflection trace steps
option.PBR_SSR_STEPS.comment=How many steps the reflection trace may take.\n\n\
	Only the material reflections use this. The water reflections keep their own \
	budget, because spending this many steps on a body of water and on every \
	smooth pixel of the screen are two very different propositions.\n\n\
	More steps find more hits and refine the ones they find better. The cost is \
	paid whether or not anything is found, which makes this the first setting to \
	lower if the reflections are too expensive.

option.PBR_SSR_ROUGHNESS=Roughness limit
option.PBR_SSR_ROUGHNESS.comment=How rough a surface may be and still have the \
	world traced into its reflection.\n\n\
	Above this the reflection is the sky term alone: a rough surface scatters the \
	reflected ray over too wide a range of directions for one sharp sample to \
	stand in for, so it would be shown a mirror image it should not have.\n\n\
	Raising it reflects the world in rougher surfaces at a higher cost, and those \
	reflections will be sharper than they should be - there is no blur behind \
	this, only a direction.

option.PBR_DEFAULT_MATERIAL=Default material
option.PBR_DEFAULT_MATERIAL.comment=Whether blocks that the resource pack gives \
	no usable specular data for should fall back to a plain dielectric.\n\n\
	Many resource packs ship normal maps for everything but specular maps for \
	only some blocks, and the blocks without one decode to "no reflectance and \
	fully rough" - which looks like the shader simply does not apply to them.\n\n\
	Turn this off to see exactly what the resource pack authored, matte blocks \
	included.

option.PBR_DEFAULT_ROUGHNESS=Default roughness
option.PBR_DEFAULT_ROUGHNESS.comment=The roughness given to those fallback \
	materials. Lower is glossier.\n\n\
	This is also what a block looks like when the resource pack ships no \
	specular data at all, so it sets the baseline shine of an ordinary block.

option.PBR_METALS=Hardcoded metals
option.PBR_METALS.comment=Whether to use the reflectance values that LabPBR \
	hardcodes for iron, gold, aluminum, chrome, copper, lead, platinum, and \
	silver.\n\n\
	With this off, those materials are treated as albedo-based metals instead, \
	which is far closer than treating them as ordinary dielectrics.

option.PBR_METAL_DIFFUSE=Metal diffuse
option.PBR_METAL_DIFFUSE.comment=How much of its diffuse response a metal loses, \
	over the direct light and the indirect light around it together.\n\n\
	A metal has no diffuse response at all in reality: light either reflects off \
	it or is absorbed, and everything that gives a metal its appearance is in the \
	reflection. A surface that keeps some of its diffuse and gains a reflection on \
	top does not look like a restrained metal, it looks like a metal with \
	something smeared over it - because that is what it is: a bright, coloured, \
	blurred film on a body that is still scattering light the way plastic does.\n\n\
	This used to default to half, on the grounds that the pack had no environment \
	reflection and a fully metallic surface would be left with its highlight alone \
	and read as a black hole in a cave. That reason is long gone: the reflection \
	has been here for a while, and a metal in a lit cave picks up the block light \
	around it through the ambient specular. The halfway value is what the smearing \
	was.\n\n\
	0.0 keeps all of the diffuse response, which looks like coloured plastic. \
	1.0 removes it entirely, which is physically correct. Lower it if metals come \
	out too dark in the dark.

option.PBR_SUBSURFACE=Subsurface scattering
option.PBR_SUBSURFACE.comment=Whether to add the light that scatters through a \
	thin surface, using the amount the resource pack stored in the blue channel \
	of the specular map.\n\n\
	LabPBR splits that channel in two: values up to 64 out of 255 are porosity, \
	and anything above that is how much light the material lets through. Leaves, \
	grass, paper, and similar thin materials are what use the upper range, and \
	what this adds is the glow of a backlit leaf - light that comes through the \
	surface instead of bouncing off it, so the brightest side is the one facing \
	away from the sun.\n\n\
	Steadfast already brightens foliage from every direction based on the block \
	being leaves or grass. This option makes the effect follow the texture \
	instead, so that a pack can mark a single leaf or a single plant as thin, and \
	it is what adds the directional glow on top.\n\n\
	A material that stores nothing in that channel is unaffected, so this cannot \
	brighten a solid block.

option.PBR_SSS_STRENGTH=Scattering strength
option.PBR_SSS_STRENGTH.comment=How much scattered light is added. 0.7 is the \
	value the effect was tuned at.\n\n\
	Raise it for a stronger glow through leaves; lower it if foliage looks like \
	it is lit from the inside rather than by the sun.

option.PBR_POROSITY_WETNESS=Wet porous surfaces
option.PBR_POROSITY_WETNESS.comment=Whether surfaces the resource pack marked as \
	porous get darker while they are wet, which is what makes brick, dirt, and \
	wood look soaked after rain rather than merely shiny.\n\n\
	LabPBR stores porosity in the lower half of the specular map's blue channel. \
	A porous material gets its light response from scattering inside it, and \
	water filling those pores takes that scattering away - so the surface \
	darkens, and what is left is the wet sheen.\n\n\
	The darkening is scaled by the sky light, so it only applies outdoors: a \
	torch-lit cellar looks the same in the rain as out of it.\n\n\
	It only shows itself in rain, and only on the materials a pack marked as \
	porous, so it costs nothing anywhere else. The Porosity / scattering channel \
	under the material debug view shows which ones those are - the dark values \
	are porosity.

option.PBR_TRANSLUCENT=PBR on ice and glass
option.PBR_TRANSLUCENT.comment=Whether ice and other translucent solids take \
	their reflectance and their normal from the resource pack.\n\n\
	Ice is drawn by the translucent pass, which gives water a reflectance of 0.1 \
	and gives everything else none at all - so an icy surface currently reflects \
	nothing except at grazing angles, no matter what the resource pack authored \
	for it. With this on, ice is decoded like any other block and the reflections \
	it already had become reflections of that material.\n\n\
	Water is deliberately not included: Steadfast's water is an animated surface \
	whose normal comes from its own wave model, and a pack's water material \
	describes a thinner and much smoother surface than the one being drawn.\n\n\
	Turn it off to see an icy biome the way the resource pack left it.

option.PBR_HAND_ITEM_MATERIALS=PBR on held items (not blocks)
option.PBR_HAND_ITEM_MATERIALS.comment=Whether a held **item** - not a held \
	block - reads the resource pack's PBR materials as well.\n\n\
	On, and it is what makes a held item look like the material it is made of: a \
	blade catches the sun, a polished tool mirrors the sky.\n\n\
	It is an option because of where that material comes from. The material maps \
	are generated for the block atlas, sprite for sprite, while an item model is \
	drawn from the item atlas, where the sprites sit elsewhere. A resource pack \
	that ships no LabPBR maps for its items leaves those coordinates reading \
	another sprite's material, or none at all - turn this off for such a pack and \
	items keep the plain colour it gave them.\n\n\
	It is not the option to reach for if a held item looks see-through. That was \
	the environment reflection being computed for the hand at all - the hand hangs \
	in front of the camera rather than standing in the world, so the traced ray \
	lands on the world behind the item and the sky reflection lands on the sky \
	behind the player. The hand is now left out of that reflection entirely, so \
	leave this on.

option.PBR_HAND_ITEMS=PBR on held items
option.PBR_HAND_ITEMS.comment=Whether the item or block in your hand gets PBR \
	materials as well.\n\n\
	A held block is drawn from the block atlas, so it carries exactly the same \
	material data as the same block placed in the world: turning this on is what \
	stops a held stone from looking flat while the stone in the wall beside it \
	does not.\n\n\
	On by default, so that the world is consistent: a held block carries the same \
	materials as the one placed next to it. A held item that is not a block reads \
	its material from the item atlas as well - see the option above.

option.PBR_ENTITIES=PBR on entities
option.PBR_ENTITIES.comment=Whether mobs and other entities get PBR materials.\n\n\
	This one is an experiment, and turning it on is how to find out whether it \
	works: the block atlas is the only atlas Iris and OptiFine are documented to \
	build normal and specular maps for, so an entity may end up shaded with some \
	arbitrary block's materials instead of its own.\n\n\
	To check: turn it on, look at a mob, and select the Occlusion channel under \
	the material debug view. A flat, uniform picture means no material data is \
	bound for that entity, and this should be turned back off. Detail that varies \
	across the mob and lines up with its own texture means it works.\n\n\
	On by default: if a resource pack ships no material maps for entities there \
	is nothing for it to read. Turn it off if the check above shows it picking up \
	materials that are not the entity's own.

option.PBR_DEBUG=Material debug view [build 261]
option.SSS_GAIN=Sky shadow depth gain
