screen.LIGHTING_BLOCK=Block light and bleed
screen.SHADOWS=Shadows
screen.DEBUG_VIEWS=Debug views
screen.DEV_TOOLS=Developer switches
option.TAA=Temporal anti-aliasing
option.CLOUD_DEBUG=Cloud lighting diagnostic [0 off | 1 direct | 2 transmittance | 3 phase]
option.CLOUD_PHASE_FLAT=Cloud direct light (flat phase value)
option.CLOUD_TRANSMITTANCE_FLAT=Cloud direct light (flat transmittance)
option.CLOUD_LAYER_FALLOFF_BASE=Light at the cloud layer's bottom
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_NONE=Off
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_HEIGHT=Height channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_SMOOTHNESS=Smoothness
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_F0=Reflectance (F0)
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_NORMAL=Normal
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_MIP=Material detail limit
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_TANGENT_NORMAL=Tangent-space normal
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_MATERIAL_AO=Occlusion channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_SUBSURFACE=Porosity / scattering channel
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_EMISSION=Emission
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_PARALLAX=Parallax displacement (diagnostic)
value.PBR_DEBUG.PBR_DEBUG_REFLECTION=Reflection alone (4x) (diagnostic)
option.PBR_DEBUG.comment=Replaces the image with a visualisation of the material \
	data, which is the quickest way to find out whether a resource pack actually \
	provides what an effect needs.\n\n\
	Look at a block with Height selected: if it comes out flat grey, that \
	resource pack has no height channel for it, and no amount of parallax tuning \
	will produce any displacement.\n\n\
	Tangent-space normal shows the normal map as the resource pack authored it, \
	before the block's orientation is applied. A flat normal is light blue; black \
	is a texel from outside the sprite, which decodes to a strong tilt rather \
	than a flat one. Compare it with Normal to tell a bad sample apart from a bad \
	tangent frame.\n\n\
	Occlusion channel is what the Material occlusion option reads. Flat white \
	means the pack ships no occlusion data for that block, and there is nothing \
	for the feature to apply there.\n\n\
	Porosity / scattering channel is the counterpart for the specular map: black \
	means the pack stores nothing in that channel, dark colours are porosity, and \
	anything brighter is the material letting light through.\n\n\
	Parallax displacement is a diagnostic rather than a material view: it draws \
	the three values the parallax ray march works with, as colours. Magenta \
	means Parallax mapping is off, since the three channels only mean anything \
	while it is on. With it on:\n\n\
	Red is how far the ray is displaced before the march runs. Black red means \
	the offset itself is near zero, which is the tangent frame, the view angle, \
	or one of the two displacement limits.\n\n\
	Green is the displacement the march actually returned. Red lit with green \
	black means the offset was fine and the march failed to find a crossing.\n\n\
	Blue is the distance fade, ie, how much of the effect this fragment is \
	allowed. Black blue means the surface is simply too far away.\n\n\
	Red and green share a scale, and that scale is the sprite itself: an offset of \
	half a block face is a full channel. That way the reading means the same \
	thing whether the pack's atlas is 256 wide or 4096 - a fixed scale leaves \
	both channels too dim to see on a large atlas. Both are square rooted as \
	well, so a small offset is still readable next to a large one.

option.GLASS_REFLECTIONS=Glass reflections
option.GLASS_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether glass and stained glass reflect the way \
	water and ice do.\n\n\
	Glass reflects about 4% of the light that lands on it head on, and nearly all \
	of it at a glancing angle - which is what a window looks like: mostly you see \
	through it, but from the side it turns into a mirror. Without this, glass has \
	no specular response at all, and a pane is a tinted hole in the world.\n\n\
	The reflections use the same code the water and ice ones do, so they include \
	the screen-space pass and therefore reflect the world rather than only the \
	sky. Like every reflection here they fade out with the sky light, so glass \
	underground and deep indoors stays unreflective.\n\n\
	Water and ice are not affected: they always reflected.

option.MOTION_BLUR=Motion blur
option.MOTION_BLUR.comment=Smears the image along the distance the camera has \
	travelled through the world, so that walking, running, falling, riding a boat \
	and being thrown all leave a trail the way they do in a camera.\n\n\
	It works per pixel: each pixel's position is recovered, the camera's travel \
	over the last frame says where that point was a frame ago, and the distance \
	between the two screen positions is how far this pixel moved. The image is then \
	averaged along that distance. Because the movement is recovered for every pixel \
	rather than once for the screen, walking makes the ground at your feet rush \
	past while the horizon barely moves - which is what a camera does and what one \
	screen-wide offset cannot.\n\n\
	Travel and turning are both used; the walk bob is not. The bob is a rotation \
	too, and it shares the camera's matrix with the turning, so the two cannot be \
	told apart by anything but their size: at a walk the bob moves the picture one \
	or two pixels a frame, while a turn worth blurring moves it dozens. So a turn \
	is only blurred past a few pixels a frame, and a slow one - a hand resting on \
	the mouse - is not. Running, falling, boats and knockback are travel, and travel \
	always blurs.\n\n\
	Only the camera is blurred: Steadfast has no motion vectors, so a mob, a river \
	or a falling block carries no record of where it was and stays sharp. The hand \
	is left out entirely - it is held in front of the camera, so on screen it is \
	the one thing that is standing still.\n\n\
	Runs in the final pass before the tonemap, so the blur mixes linear light and \
	the godrays stay crisp.

option.MOTION_BLUR_AMOUNT=Blur amount
option.MOTION_BLUR_AMOUNT.comment=How long the blur is, as a multiple of the \
	distance the pixel actually moved.\n\n\
	1.0 is the honest length. Lower is a hint of movement; higher runs longer than \
	the movement that caused it, which reads as faster and less true.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Motion blur is off.

option.MOTION_BLUR_SAMPLES=Samples
option.MOTION_BLUR_SAMPLES.comment=How many samples are taken along that \
	movement, and so how smooth the blur is.\n\n\
	Each one is a texture fetch on every pixel of the screen, which is what this \
	feature costs: 2 shows as a visible double image, 8 is smooth in most scenes, \
	and past that the difference is small.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Motion blur is off.

option.MOTION_BLUR_LENS=Fun: the blur lens (an old bug)
option.MOTION_BLUR_LENS.comment=Keeps a bug this effect used to have, exactly as \
	it was, because it looks like something. Off by default.\n\n\
	The bug was in the turning half. Measuring a turn means taking this pixel's \
	direction and putting it through the view the previous frame was drawn with, \
	and the direction has to be turned into world axes first. That step was left \
	out, so the view-space direction went straight into the previous view's matrix \
	and the camera's own rotation was applied twice. What that draws is a disc of \
	movement nobody made, turning with the camera at twice the angle: looking north \
	it sits where you are looking and does nothing, and a quarter turn of the view \
	carries it half a turn round.\n\n\
	Has no effect while Motion blur is off. Turning this on replaces the turning \
	blur with the lens rather than adding to it.
value.STARS.STARS_OFF=Off
value.STARS.STARS_ON=On
value.TAA.TAA_OFF=Off
value.TAA.TAA_ON=On
option.TAA.comment=**On by default.** It is the one effect here that trades image \
	stability for smoothness, and the trade is worth it at these settings: it \
	renders each frame with a small sub-pixel offset and \
	averages several frames together, so that each pixel is sampled from \
	several positions instead of one. This smooths out edges that no single \
	frame can resolve, for far less than rendering at a higher resolution.\n\n\
	Steadfast has no motion vectors, so things that move on their own - \
	entities, water, particles, the held item - cannot be reprojected correctly \
	and are handled by throwing away history that does not match the current \
	frame instead. Lower the strength - or turn this off entirely - if the \
	ghosting behind moving things reads worse than the aliasing it removes.\n\n\
	Voxy terrain is not covered, as its geometry is emitted by the Voxy mod \
	rather than by these vertex shaders.

option.TAA_STRENGTH=Anti-aliasing strength
option.TAA_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the accumulated history each frame \
	keeps, for a pixel that did not move.\n\n\
	This is the option that decides how much of the noise in the image averages \
	away - the dither of the screen-space shadows in the distance, and the sky's \
	own. A longer history leaves less of both and resolves more detail, because \
	averaging consecutive frames is the only thing that can remove a per-frame \
	sample at all.\n\n\
	A pixel that did move keeps seven tenths of this, which is what lets the \
	default sit high: raising it buys noise reduction while standing still \
	without buying ghosting behind moving objects.

option.TAA_JITTER=Jitter radius
option.TAA_JITTER.comment=The radius of the sub-pixel offset, in pixels.\n\n\
	One pixel places each frame's sample anywhere within the pixel, which is \
	what an anti-aliasing filter wants. 0 turns the offset off, which leaves \
	the pass running but with nothing to average.

option.TAA_CLAMP=History clamp
option.TAA_CLAMP.comment=How many standard deviations of the current frame's \
	neighbourhood the history is allowed to fall outside of.\n\n\
	This is what stops a moving object from dragging its old positions along \
	behind it, and what keeps textures from being averaged into a smudge. Raise \
	it if the image flickers, lower it if surfaces look soft or smeared.

option.TAA_SHARPEN=Sharpening
option.TAA_SHARPEN.comment=How much to sharpen the resolved image, to counter \
	the softening that averaging frames together introduces.\n\n\
	A little goes a long way - too much brings back the aliasing the effect \
	exists to remove.

option.PBR_EMISSION=Emission
option.PBR_EMISSION.comment=Whether the emission stored in the alpha channel of \
	the specular map should be added to the surface color.\n\n\
	Emissive blocks then glow with their own color regardless of the light \
	around them, and still fade into the fog with distance.

option.PBR_EMISSION_STRENGTH=Emission strength
option.PBR_EMISSION_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the emission from the resource \
	pack is.\n\n\
	LabPBR stores it as a fraction of the surface color, which on its own is \
	rarely bright enough to look like it is lit from within, since the \
	tonemapper compresses it straight back down.

option.BLOCK_EMISSION=Block self-emission
option.BLOCK_EMISSION.comment=Whether light sources glow with their own color: \
	glowstone, sea lanterns, lava, torches, and so on.\n\n\
	Unlike the emission above, this needs nothing from the resource pack - it \
	uses the marker that Iris and OptiFine put in the lightmap for faces that \
	can emit light.\n\n\
	This lights up the block itself, not its surroundings; the light it casts \
	on its neighbours is already part of the block lighting.

option.BLOCK_EMISSION_STRENGTH=Self-emission strength
option.BLOCK_EMISSION_STRENGTH.comment=How bright block self-emission is.\n\n\
	Values above 1.0 push the surface past the white point of the tonemapper, \
	which is what a light source should look like.

option.BLOCKLIGHT_STRENGTH=Block light strength
option.BLOCKLIGHT_STRENGTH.comment=How bright block light is at full light level.\n\n\
	The color of block light now comes from the lightmap itself, which is how a \
	resource pack says what color its lights are, rather than from a color this \
	pack picked. What the lightmap cannot answer is how bright block light \
	should be here, because it has its own falloff baked into it and this model \
	replaces that with its own - so that is what this is.\n\n\
	Raising it brightens everything a torch lights; it does not change its \
	color.

option.LIGHT_TINT_STRENGTH=Light color tinting
option.LIGHT_TINT_STRENGTH.comment=How far a surface takes the color of the \
	light sources near it, rather than merely having their light added on top of \
	its own color.\n\n\
	Adding light alone cannot turn an orange-lit wall blue: the wall already has \
	a strong orange under it, and pouring blue on top of that gives a paler \
	orange rather than a blue wall. This pulls the surface's own color towards \
	the color of the light around it at the same time, which is what a surface \
	lit by a blue light actually does - it reflects mostly blue.\n\n\
	The effect fades out with distance from the sources on its own, because it \
	is driven by how much light source is nearby. Raise it for stronger color, \
	lower it to get closer to how the pack looked before.

option.MIN_AMBIENT_BRIGHTNESS=Minimum ambient brightness
option.MIN_AMBIENT_BRIGHTNESS.comment=A floor under the ambient light: what \
	every surface is lit with regardless of whether anything is lighting \
	it.\n\n\
	Steadfast's ambient light is driven by sky light, which leaves anywhere \
	without a light source of its own - the inside of a cave, the Nether, and \
	the End, which has no sky light at all - very close to black.\n\n\
	The values are linear light, so they look small: the default of 0.1 is \
	several times the floor the pack has without it. Raise it until dark places \
	are readable; set it back to 0.0 for exactly the pack's original look.

option.COLORED_LIGHTS=Colored lights
option.COLORED_LIGHTS.comment=Whether light sources take the color of the light \
	they actually give off - a soul lantern's blue, a redstone torch's red - \
	instead of the one warm orange the pack uses for all block light.\n\n\
	Minecraft tells a shader how much light arrived at a position, but not what \
	color it was or which block it came from, so this is done in two parts. The \
	block that is glowing takes the color of its own light, which is the one \
	place where the source is known. The surfaces around it are then lit by a \
	screen-space bleed, which spreads the colors of the light sources that are \
	on screen onto what is near them.\n\n\
	Turning this off removes both, and skips the bleed pass entirely, so it is \
	also the cheapest setting.

option.COLORED_LIGHTS_STRENGTH=Light source color
option.COLORED_LIGHTS_STRENGTH.comment=How far a light source's own surface \
	takes the color of its light rather than the color of its texture.\n\n\
	At 1.0 the emitter is lit purely by its light's color; the brightness \
	pattern of the texture is kept either way, so a torch still reads as a \
	torch rather than as a flat orange rectangle. Lower this if a resource \
	pack's textures carry detail on their glowing parts that you would rather \
	keep.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_STRENGTH=Light bleed strength
option.LIGHT_BLEED_STRENGTH.comment=How much light the sources on screen spread \
	onto the surfaces around them.\n\n\
	This is what makes a soul lantern tint the wall beside it blue, and lava \
	tint the stone around it orange. It adds light rather than recoloring what \
	is already there, so a surface that block light has already lit strongly \
	orange keeps that orange underneath - raise this until the color reads, and \
	lower it if surfaces near a source look washed out.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_RADIUS=Light bleed radius
option.LIGHT_BLEED_RADIUS.comment=How far the color spreads, in pixels at \
	1080p, scaled to your resolution.\n\n\
	This costs nothing extra, as the number of samples is fixed. Very large \
	values spread a source's color across most of the screen, where it stops \
	reading as light coming from that source.

option.LIGHT_BLEED_DEPTH=Light bleed depth tolerance
option.LIGHT_BLEED_DEPTH.comment=How different in depth two pixels may be \
	before light stops spreading between them.\n\n\
	Without this, a light source would bleed straight through a wall onto \
	whatever is in front of it. Raise it if the color stops too abruptly at the \
	edges of objects; lower it if a light seems to shine through the wall it is \
	behind.

option.END_SKY=End sky
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_AUTO=End only
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_ALWAYS=Everywhere
value.END_SKY.END_SKY_OFF=Off
option.END_SKY.comment=Whether the End gets a sky of its own: open space with a \
	starfield and a body of its own in it, rather than the Overworld's atmosphere \
	model, which has a sun and a horizon that the End does not have.\n\n\
	End only picks it by dimension, which is what you want. Everywhere is a \
	test: it draws the same sky in the Overworld and the Nether, which is the \
	quickest way to confirm the sky itself works if you are not seeing it in \
	the End.\n\n\
	The End has no sky light at all, so its islands are lit only by the Minimum \
	ambient brightness option above; raise that if the End is too dark to \
	navigate.

option.END_STAR_DENSITY=Star density
option.END_STAR_DENSITY.comment=How crowded the End's starfield is. At 0.0 the \
	sky is empty, and each step above the default roughly doubles the number of \
	stars drawn.

option.END_STAR_BRIGHTNESS=Star brightness
option.END_STAR_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright the stars are.\n\n\
	They are the only thing in the End that is brighter than the surface of a \
	lit block, so this sets how much the sky comes to dominate the view.

option.END_BODY=The End's body
value.END_BODY.END_BODY_OFF=Off
value.END_BODY.END_BODY_STAR=Star
value.END_BODY.END_BODY_BLACK_HOLE=Black hole
option.END_BODY.comment=What to put at the point in the End's sky that the \
	dimension is lit from.\n\n\
	The End has no sky light, so the only thing lighting its islands is this \
	pack's direct light, and in the End that light comes from a fixed direction - \
	the sun sits where noon puts it and stays there, tilted by the pack's sun \
	path rotation. That is the one place in this sky where something belongs, so \
	whichever is chosen here is drawn exactly where that light comes from, above \
	the light it casts.\n\n\
	Star: a disc with a boiling surface and a corona. This is the one that makes \
	sense of the lighting, since the light is coming from it.\n\n\
	Black hole: a black shadow, a ring of light bent around its edge, and an \
	accretion disc that is brighter on the side coming towards you. The disc \
	turns at a speed that falls with distance, so it shears rather than rotating \
	as a rigid texture, and the sky around the hole is bent so that what is \
	behind it is seen around it. It is nonsense as a light source and looks the \
	best.\n\n\
	Off leaves the stars on their own.

option.END_AMBIENT=End light
option.END_AMBIENT.comment=How much light the End has of its own.\n\n\
	Every other dimension is lit by its sky, and the End has no sky light at \
	all, so without this its islands are lit by nothing but the Minimum ambient \
	brightness above - which is to say, almost not at all. This stands in for \
	the light the End's sky does not give it, and it is a violet-white light \
	rather than a neutral one, so that the End reads as somewhere else rather \
	than as an unlit overworld.\n\n\
	Off by default. It was reported as having no effect at all where it was \
	written, and the cause was never found. What is known: the End's sky and \
	haze do work there, so the dimension is recognized, and the debug view's \
	version of this light - ten times brighter and ignoring this value - does \
	show up. That leaves the value itself reaching the terrain as the only \
	question, which points at the terrain rather than at this option: terrain \
	drawn by another mod, Voxy for instance, is rendered by a shader path that \
	is given no biome information and would take itself for the overworld.\n\n\
	Raise it to try it; around 0.35 is a readable but still very dim End.
option.END_VOID_GLOW=Void glow
option.END_VOID_GLOW.comment=How much light comes up out of the void below the \
	End's islands.\n\n\
	It lights the undersides of everything, which is what stops the islands \
	from reading as flat cutouts, and it costs nothing anywhere else.\n\n\
	Off by default, for the same reason as the light above.

option.END_FOG=End haze
option.END_FOG.comment=How far the End's haze takes the color of its sky.\n\n\
	The End has no sky light, so the fog treats it as one enormous cave and \
	fogs it with the cave color. This fogs it with the color of its sky instead, \
	which is what lets its islands fade into space rather than into a grey \
	wall.\n\n\
	At 0.0 the End is fogged exactly as it was before.

option.END_DEBUG=End detection debug view
option.END_DEBUG.comment=Replaces the sky with a report of what the checks that \
	decide whether this is the End actually returned.\n\n\
	There are two ways to ask, not every version provides both, and there is no \
	way to tell from here: a uniform the shader mod does not provide reads as 0, \
	which looks exactly like "not the End". This turns the answer into a color, \
	in the sky, where it is easy to see:\n\n\
	red - the dimension uniform says the End\ngreen - the biome category says \
	the End\nblue - the pack concluded it is the End\n\n\
	So in the End: white means both work, magenta or cyan means only one does, \
	and black means neither does - in which case setting End sky to Everywhere \
	forces the effect on. In the overworld red and green should be black and the \
	sky should look normal.\n\n\
	It also paints, in the End, the things each program draws, which is how the \
	End's light flash can be traced to the program responsible for it:\n\n\
	red - the terrain: the End's own light reaching it\ngreen - glowing parts \
	(eyes)\nblue - the enchant glint\nwhite - the beacon beam\nblack - lines and \
	the block outline\nmagenta - weather and entities\norange - block \
	entities\nteal - water and translucents\npurple - translucent entities\n\
	yellow - the held item\ngrey - the block breaking overlay\n\n\
	If the patch of block texture in the sky turns one of those colors, that is \
	the program drawing it. If the terrain turns red, the End's light is working \
	and the sliders under Lighting are doing something.\n\n\
	This is a diagnostic, and is off by default.

option.HIDE_END_FLASH=Hide the End's light flash
option.HIDE_END_FLASH.comment=Whether to drop the light flash that Minecraft \
	1.21.9 and up draws in the End's sky.\n\n\
	It does not survive being shadered: it is a textured quad that the shader \
	mod has no texture binding for, so it ends up sampling whatever was left \
	bound - the block atlas - and appears as a patch of some random block's \
	texture hanging in the sky. There is nothing to be done with it from here, \
	since it is not this pack's sky and cannot be lit by it, so it is dropped \
	instead.\n\n\
	On versions without the flash this does nothing at all, which is checked \
	for rather than assumed. Turn it off if you would rather keep the flash.

option.FANCY_TRANSLUCENTS=Fancy translucents
option.FANCY_TRANSLUCENTS.comment=Whether water, ice, and glass get their own \
	shading: reflections, refraction, and the absorption that darkens things with \
	depth.\n\n\
	With it off, translucent surfaces are shaded exactly like solid blocks - \
	faster, and closer to how they looked before any of those effects existed, but \
	a window and a pool of water both turn into flat sheets of colour.

option.VANILLA_ISH_WATER=Vanilla-style water
option.VANILLA_ISH_WATER.comment=Whether the water surface is drawn with the \
	water texture and vanilla's flat alpha, instead of being tinted towards the \
	sky colour and faded by the strength of its reflection.\n\n\
	Off - the default - is what makes water calm down into a reflective surface \
	where the sky reaches it and turn opaque where it does not.

option.SCREENSPACE_REFLECTIONS=Screen-space reflections
option.SCREENSPACE_REFLECTIONS.comment=Whether water, ice, and glass may reflect \
	the world and not only the sky, by tracing the reflected ray through the depth \
	buffer.\n\n\
	A reflection can only show what is already on screen, so the edges of the \
	view and anything hidden behind the surface itself fall back to the sky. Water \
	hides that with its waves; a mirror-like surface does not, which is why this is \
	a compromise rather than a correct reflection.

option.SCREENSPACE_REFRACTION=Screen-space refraction
option.SCREENSPACE_REFRACTION.comment=Whether what is seen through a water \
	surface is displaced by its waves, based on how deep the water is behind it.\n\n\
	This is deliberately not physically correct: real refraction moves objects \
	far enough to be disorienting to play with, and needs information that is not \
	on the screen. What it does instead is move the background around a little, \
	which is enough to read as water without making it hard to see through.

option.WATER_PARALLAX=Water parallax
option.WATER_PARALLAX.comment=Displaces the sampled water surface along the view \
	ray by the height of its waves, so that ripples have visible depth when seen \
	at a glancing angle instead of looking painted onto a flat sheet.\n\n\
	Only applies to the noise water surface, which is the one that has waves to \
	displace by.

option.WATER_PARALLAX_DISTANCE=Water parallax distance
option.WATER_PARALLAX_DISTANCE.comment=Distance in meters at which water parallax \
	fades out.\n\n\
	Past a certain distance one pixel covers more than a whole wave, so the \
	effect stops describing anything and only costs samples - which is what this \
	fades it away before.

option.WATER_AA=Water antialiasing
option.WATER_AA.comment=How much the wave normals are smoothed, which is what \
	keeps distant water from shimmering as the fine waves fall below one pixel.\n\n\
	Smoothing them also flattens the water, so this trades sparkle against detail: \
	Off keeps every wave and its aliasing, High is the calmest and the least \
	detailed.

option.NUM_CRESTS=Wave crests
option.NUM_CRESTS.comment=How many crest waves the water surface is built from, \
	which are the large, slow shapes that give it its form. Noise water only.\n\n\
	More crests make a choppier sea at a higher cost.

option.NUM_RIPPLES=Wave ripples
option.NUM_RIPPLES.comment=How many ripple waves are added on top of the crests, \
	which are the fine, fast detail. Noise water only.

option.NUM_CAUSTICS=Caustic waves
option.NUM_CAUSTICS.comment=How many waves the underwater caustic pattern is \
	built from.\n\n\
	Each one adds another set of bright lines to the pattern, so lowering this \
	makes caustics cheaper and blobbier.

option.WATER_SURFACE=Water surface
option.WATER_SURFACE.comment=Which model the water surface uses.\n\n\
	Noise generates its waves procedurally and works out the normal from their \
	slopes, which is smooth and is what every other water option here builds on. \
	Basic is a flat surface with no waves at all - the cheapest, and the one to \
	pick if the water is causing trouble.

option.WATER_CAUSTICS=Water caustics
option.WATER_CAUSTICS.comment=The moving pattern of light that sunlit water \
	casts onto the terrain below it, brightened where the light is focused.\n\n\
	It is read out of the shadow map, which only covers as far as the shadow \
	distance and only knows the water depth where the water surface is itself in \
	the shadow map, so caustics fade out at that range rather than being drawn \
	wrongly past it.

option.WATER_SCATTER=Water scattering
option.WATER_SCATTER.comment=The light the water itself scatters back out, added \
to whatever comes through it.\n\n\
Absorption on its own can only take colour away, so it gives water its deep \
blue-green but cannot make a lit body of water glow. This is the other half. \
Zero leaves the water exactly as it was, which is why that is the default: \
raising it is a change to the water's colour, so it is a choice.
option.WATER_SCATTER_MAGNITUDE=Water scattering magnitude
option.WATER_SCATTER_MAGNITUDE.comment=The same effect's scale, kept apart so \
that the slider above can stay in the range a person thinks in.
option.WATER_SCATTER_LUMINANCE=Water scattering brightness
option.WATER_SCATTER_LUMINANCE.comment=How bright the scattered light is at full \
depth under a full sky. Zero leaves the effect switched off whatever the sliders \
above say.
option.WATER_CHARACTER=Water colour
option.WATER_CHARACTER.comment=How quickly water takes different colours of light \
	away with depth, which is what decides whether a body of water reads as \
	tropical, as cold, or as neither.\n\n\
	Cold takes the red out first, leaving the blue-green of deep northern water. \
	Tropical takes nearly everything but blue, which is what makes shallow warm \
	water glow. Balanced sits between them.

option.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD=Water absorption
option.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD.comment=How light is absorbed by water.\n\n\
	Refraction assisted applies it through the refraction pass, where the water \
	surface can be seen through, and falls back to a sky light estimate for scenes \
	that are entirely underwater. No absorption switches it off altogether, which \
	is what to use if the water looks wrong for a particular resource pack or mod \
	that draws its own.

value.WATER_AA.OFF=Off
value.WATER_AA.LOW=Low
value.WATER_AA.BALANCED=Balanced
value.WATER_AA.HIGH=High

value.WATER_SURFACE.BASIC=Basic
value.WATER_SURFACE.NOISE=Noise

value.WATER_CHARACTER.COLD=Cold
value.WATER_CHARACTER.BALANCED=Balanced
value.WATER_CHARACTER.TROPICAL=Tropical

value.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD.NO_ABSORPTION=No absorption
value.WATER_ABSORPTION_METHOD.REFRACTION_ASSISTED=Refraction assisted

option.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING=Day sky lighting
option.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.comment=The style of direct and ambient sky lighting used \
	during the day.\n\n\
	The three styles match the atmosphere presets, so pick the one that goes with \
	the sky above you: Semi-natural keeps the sun's light close to white and its \
	shadows natural, Fantasy warms and saturates it, and Retro flattens it towards \
	the look of a much older shader.

option.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING=Night sky lighting
option.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.comment=The style of direct and ambient sky lighting \
	used at night, matching the atmosphere presets the same way the day setting \
	does. The sun is replaced by the moon, so this decides how blue and how bright \
	a moonlit night is.

value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural
value.DAY_SKY_LIGHTING.RETRO=Retro

value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.FANTASY=Fantasy
value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.SEMI_NATURAL=Semi-natural
value.NIGHT_SKY_LIGHTING.RETRO=Retro

option.HELD_BLOCK_LIGHTING=Held light
option.HELD_BLOCK_LIGHTING.comment=Whether the block or item in your hand lights \
	the world around you, as if you were carrying it as a lamp.\n\n\
	This is on top of the light Minecraft gives the block itself: a torch in your \
	hand lights the ground at your feet here, where vanilla would only light the \
	torch.

option.SOFTER_SHADOWS=Softer shadows
option.SOFTER_SHADOWS.comment=Whether the shadow map is sampled four times \
	instead of once, each of those being a hardware-filtered 2x2 tap - sixteen \
	texels in all.\n\n\
	This is what keeps a shadow's edge from looking like a staircase, and it costs \
	three extra taps.

option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS=Shadow softness
option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.comment=How far apart the four shadow taps are placed. \
	Higher is softer and blurrier.\n\n\
	At the low end the taps overlap and the extra samples stop buying anything, so \
	there is nothing to gain by lowering it below the smallest steps.

option.SHADOW_DISTORT_FACTOR=Shadow distortion factor
option.SHADOW_DISTORT_FACTOR.comment=How far the shadow sampling position is \
	pushed along the surface normal, on faces that point away from the light.\n\n\
	This is the fixed part of the shadow bias, and it is what decides whether \
	shadows hold tight against the objects casting them. Lower it and contact \
	shadows get closer and darker; raise it if surfaces show shadows they should \
	not be casting on themselves.

option.shadowMapResolution=Shadow map resolution
option.shadowMapResolution.comment=The size of the shadow map, which decides how \
	much detail a shadow can resolve.\n\n\
	Higher is sharper and costs both memory and fill rate, since the whole shadow \
	pass is redrawn at this size. If shadows look blocky at their edges, this is \
	the setting that fixes it - softness only blurs what resolution has already \
	resolved.

option.REAL_TIME_SHADOWS=Real-time shadows
option.REAL_TIME_SHADOWS.comment=Whether terrain, entities, and block entities \
	cast shadows through the shadow map.\n\n\
	With it off, only the static light map shadows remain: much cheaper, and \
	correct for enclosed spaces, but nothing moving casts anything and the sun \
	stops being something you can stand behind.

option.COLORED_SHADOWS=Coloured shadows
option.COLORED_SHADOWS.comment=Whether the light that has come through stained \
	glass arrives with the glass's colour in it, so that a red pane lays a red \
	patch of sunlight on the floor rather than a hole in it.\n\n\
	Stained glass does not block the light in the first place - it is drawn in the \
	translucent pass, which the shadow map leaves out - so what it casts is a \
	colour and nothing else. The patch under a window is as bright as the ground \
	around it; only its hue differs. That is also why nothing here makes a stained \
	glass window's own shadow any darker or lighter than it already was.\n\n\
	The colour is read from the glass's own texture, which is what a resource pack \
	colours in the first place, and it is applied to sunlight and moonlight alone: \
	the sun is what shines through a window, and the light in the room did not \
	come through it.\n\n\
	The buffer this travels in clears to white and is written white by everything \
	that is not stained glass, so a scene with no stained glass in it multiplies \
	its direct light by exactly one. Turning this off costs a little less than \
	leaving it on, and looks precisely as the pack did before it existed.

option.COLORED_SHADOWS_STRENGTH=Coloured shadow strength
option.COLORED_SHADOWS_STRENGTH.comment=How much of the colour the light takes \
	on, where 0.0 takes none of it and 1.0 gives the light the full colour of its \
	source. It applies to stained glass and to the nether portal alike.\n\n\
	At 1.0 the patch under a window is the colour the glass is, which means a pane \
	of black stained glass puts the floor under it in the dark. That is what black \
	glass does and it is why this is a setting rather than a constant - lower it \
	towards 0.0 to keep the light and take only the hue, which is closer to how \
	Mellow and Sundial read.

option.COLORED_SHADOWS_PORTAL=Nether portal glow
option.COLORED_SHADOWS_PORTAL.comment=Whether a nether portal tints the light \
	around it, so that the ground in front of one is lit violet rather than with \
	plain sunlight.\n\n\
	A portal is not a filter the way a pane of stained glass is. It is a light \
	source standing in a frame: nothing the sunlight reaches came through it, and \
	its texture animates, so the colour is chosen here rather than read off the \
	texture - the same way this pack decides what colour a torch or a soul lantern \
	lights the world with, rather than taking a torch's colour from a texture that \
	is mostly a brown stick.\n\n\
	The colour is a violet whose brightest channel is left at full strength, so a \
	portal never dims the ground it lights: what it does is take the green out of \
	the sunlight and leave the rest, which reads as the portal's glow lying on the \
	ground. It is deliberately not drawn on translucent surfaces, which is what \
	stops a portal from colouring its own faces, and what keeps standing inside one \
	from tinting the view.\n\n\
	Turn this off to keep the portal exactly as it was and still have stained glass \
	colour its light.

option.DIRECTIONAL_SKYLIGHT_SHADING=Directional sky light
option.DIRECTIONAL_SKYLIGHT_SHADING.comment=Whether the sky's ambient light \
	depends on which way a surface faces, making an upward face brighter than a \
	downward one.\n\n\
	With it off, every face of a block receives the same amount of sky light, \
	which is the flatter and more stylised of the two and the one to pick if the \
	undersides of things look too dark.

option.GODRAYS=Volumetric light
option.GODRAYS.comment=Whether light shafts are drawn radiating from the sun, \
	from a mask of where the sky itself is visible.\n\n\
	This is a screen-space effect built by blurring that mask outwards, so it only \
	knows about what is on screen: something that should block a shaft but is off \
	the edge of the view does not.\n\n\
	Under water that mask is empty, because the surface of the water hides the sky \
	completely when it is seen from below - see Underwater light shafts for what is \
	done about it.

option.GODRAYS_STRENGTH=Volumetric light strength
option.GODRAYS_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the light shafts are, as a multiple of \
	what the pack draws on its own.\n\n\
	This one covers both sides of the waterline at once. The underwater shafts have \
	a strength of their own as well, and the two multiply: what is drawn under water \
	is this setting times that one, and what is drawn above it is only this one. \
	Setting either of them to 0.0 turns off the part it covers.\n\n\
	At 1.0 the picture is the pack's own, unchanged. The value is multiplied in \
	last, so at 1.0 all the arithmetic does is multiply by one.\n\n\
	At 0.0 the exposure is exactly zero and nothing is added: the final pass skips \
	the shafts and the blur that goes with them. That is a frame with no shafts in \
	it, the same as with Volumetric light turned off. It is not quite the same work, \
	though - turning that option off also skips the pass that builds the mask, while \
	a strength of zero leaves that pass running and writing zeros into a buffer that \
	nothing then reads.

option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS=Underwater light shafts
option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS.comment=Whether the light shafts are drawn when the \
	camera is in water as well as when it is in air.\n\n\
	Above the waterline the shafts come from a mask of where the sky is visible. \
	Under water there is no sky to be seen at all: the surface is drawn into the \
	depth that mask is built from, and from below it is a plane covering the whole \
	of the upper view, so the mask is empty and the shafts disappear entirely. This \
	puts them back by asking the same question of the water instead - what the \
	light comes through is the surface, and what gets in the way is the terrain.\n\n\
	The shafts run along the sun's own direction down there, the same axis as above \
	the waterline, so they meet the sun that is actually drawn in the sky. Light \
	really does bend as it enters water and would come down steeper, but the sun \
	here is drawn where it is without that bend: refracting the shafts on their own \
	leaves the two pointing at different places.\n\n\
	Needs Volumetric light to be on: with that off there are no shafts to draw on \
	either side of the waterline. This applies only in water; nothing is drawn in \
	lava or in powder snow.

option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS_STRENGTH=Underwater light shaft strength
option.UNDERWATER_GODRAYS_STRENGTH.comment=How bright the shafts under water are, \
	relative to the ones above the waterline at the same time of day.\n\n\
	This multiplies with Volumetric light strength, which covers both sides of the \
	waterline at once: what is drawn under water is the two of them together, and \
	what is drawn above it is only the other one. Setting either of them to 0.0 \
	turns off the part it covers.\n\n\
	At 1.0 the shafts under water are as bright as the ones above it, and an \
	above-water frame is exactly what it would have been had nothing been added \
	under the waterline at all.

option.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS=Enchant glint brightness
option.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.comment=How bright the moving sparkle on an enchanted \
	item is.\n\n\
	This is about the glint layer rather than about the item: how bright the item \
	itself is comes from its texture and the light falling on it, and this \
	changes the enchantment's sparkle drawn over the top of it.\n\n\
	It applies in all three places at once, because one program draws all three: \
	the item in your hand in first person, the item a player is holding in third \
	person, and the armour they are wearing. The shader mod has a single glint \
	program and no separate one for the hand, so there is no way to change one \
	of those and leave the others alone.\n\n\
	An item with no enchantment is not affected at all: no enchantment means \
	that program is never called, so not a pixel of it is touched.\n\n\
	1.0 is the glint exactly as this pack draws it without this option, and it \
	is the first value in the list, so this can always be put back to no effect \
	at all.\n\n\
	It is stronger than it looks: the game blends the glint with SRC_COLOR, so \
	the light it adds grows with the square of this number, and 2.0 adds four \
	times what the glint adds on its own. It scales the whole layer by the same \
	amount and leaves the pattern's own contrast alone, so what you get is a \
	brighter sparkle rather than a flat white smear. Step down if the item's own \
	surface washes out around the streaks, step up if it cannot be seen at all \
	in daylight.

value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.1.0=Off (vanilla)
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.1.5=Subtle
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.2.0=Bright (default)
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.3.0=Strong
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.4.0=Very strong
value.GLINT_BRIGHTNESS.6.0=Extreme

option.NIGHT_DESATURATION_EFFECT=Night desaturation
option.NIGHT_DESATURATION_EFFECT.comment=Drains the colour out of surfaces as \
	the light around them goes away, which is roughly what an eye does in the \
	dark.\n\n\
	It is applied per surface from its own sky and block light rather than to the \
	screen as a whole, so a torch in a dark room stays fully coloured.

option.SATURATED_TONEMAP=Saturated tonemap
option.SATURATED_TONEMAP.comment=A tonemapping curve that holds on to colour as \
	things brighten, instead of letting them wash towards white.\n\n\
	This makes sunsets, stained glass, and light sources more colourful, and costs \
	some detail in the brightest highlights to do it.

option.DAY_ATMOSPHERE=Day atmosphere
option.DAY_ATMOSPHERE.comment=The sky and sunlight preset used during the day.\n\n\
	Each one changes the sky gradient, the colour of the sun, and how the \
	atmosphere thickens with distance, so of everything here this decides what the \
	world looks like more than any other setting. It is also what the sky lighting \
	styles above are matched to.

option.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE=Night atmosphere
option.NIGHT_ATMOSPHERE.comment=The sky and moonlight preset used at night, \
	chosen separately from the day one so that a bright day and a dark night can \
	be had at the same time.

option.HARDER_HORIZON_DAY=Harder horizon (day)
option.HARDER_HORIZON_DAY.comment=Whether the sky loses its soft gradient near \
	the horizon and becomes a sharper band of colour. This goes with the Retro \
	atmospheres; with the others it mostly just looks wrong.

option.HARDER_HORIZON_NIGHT=Harder horizon (night)
option.HARDER_HORIZON_NIGHT.comment=The same for the night sky. It is set \
	separately because a sharp horizon behind the moon and a soft one behind a \
	low sun are wanted at different times.

option.BORDER_FOG=Border fog
option.BORDER_FOG.comment=Fades the far edge of the render distance into the sky \
	colour, so that the world does not simply stop at a hard line where it is cut \
	off.\n\n\
	It is fog laid over the distance rather than anything the terrain does, so it \
	also covers the gap that Distant Horizons terrain would otherwise leave.

option.ATMOSPHERE_FOG_STRENGTH_NOON=Atmosphere thickness
option.ATMOSPHERE_FOG_STRENGTH_NOON.comment=How thick the atmosphere is at noon, \
	which is what decides how much the distance is tinted and washed out.\n\n\
	Lower keeps distant terrain clearer and more contrasty, which some prefer for \
	building; higher is what makes a landscape recede convincingly.

option.PLANAR_CLOUD_LAYERS=Planar clouds
option.PLANAR_CLOUD_LAYERS.comment=Draws the clouds as flat layers of a \
	procedural texture instead of Minecraft's own boxes.\n\n\
	This is a different kind of cloud rather than a prettier version of the same \
	one: it lets them be thicker and softer, lit by the sun, and lit from \
	underneath at sunset.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUDS=Blocky volumetric clouds
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUDS.comment=Draws this pack's own cloud layer - a deck of \
	cube-shaped cloud cells, twelve blocks on a side - in place of Minecraft's \
	flat cloud boxes, and switches those off while it is on.\n\n\
	The layer is marched along the view ray in the deferred pass, which is the \
	first point at which the depth buffer is complete. That is what lets the \
	terrain hide the clouds behind it rather than the clouds drawing over it. A \
	cell is a real box, so the layer has a top and a bottom, its underside is \
	darker than its top, and a cloud standing between you and the sun has a \
	bright rim.\n\n\
	The cost is one noise fetch per cloud cell the view ray crosses, up to the \
	step count below, on every pixel of sky - and a few more on the clouds \
	themselves, for the light that reaches them. This is the most expensive \
	thing in the pack's sky, and the step count is what to lower first. The \
	layer costs nothing on the ground: it does not cast a shadow.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_CELL_SIZE=Cloud size
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_CELL_SIZE.comment=How big one cloud is, in blocks: the \
	size of a cell, and the size the noise pattern is stretched to, so it sets \
	both how chunky the cubes are and how large the masses they form are.\n\n\
	Smaller values break the layer up into many small cubes with more sky \
	between them; larger values give fewer, bigger clouds. Larger is also \
	cheaper, since the view ray crosses fewer cells on its way through.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_COVERAGE=Cloud coverage
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_COVERAGE.comment=How much of the sky the layer covers, \
	from a nearly empty sky at the bottom to an overcast one at the top. 0.5 is \
	the value it was tuned at.\n\n\
	The weather thickens the layer on its own whatever this is set to, and it \
	does so slowly: the clouds follow the wetness the game tracks rather than \
	the rain itself, so they fill in and clear out over the course of a minute \
	instead of changing the moment it starts or stops raining.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_HEIGHT=Cloud height
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_HEIGHT.comment=The height of the underside of the layer, \
	in blocks. 192 is where Minecraft's own clouds are in 1.18 and up, so that \
	turning the vanilla clouds back on puts them in the same place.\n\n\
	Raising it sends the layer further towards the horizon and covers less of the \
	sky; lowering it brings it down into the terrain, where on a mountain you can \
	end up under it or inside it.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_THICKNESS=Cloud thickness
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_THICKNESS.comment=How deep the layer is, in blocks.\n\n\
	This is what a sight line travels through inside a cloud, so it scales how \
	opaque the clouds are: thin ones read as haze, thick ones as solid. The gap \
	between the top and the bottom of the layer is also what the lighting is \
	worked out from, so a layer that is too thin looks flat no matter what the \
	lighting is set to.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_SPEED=Cloud speed
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_SPEED.comment=How fast the layer drifts, as a multiple \
	of the same wind the planar clouds move with. 0.0 stops it, which is useful \
	for looking at one shape without it moving.

option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_STEPS=Cloud march steps
option.VOLUMETRIC_CLOUD_STEPS.comment=How many cloud cells the view ray may \
	cross before the march gives up.\n\n\
	Empty sky is walked a cell at a time, so this is a cost paid by every pixel \
	of sky whether or not there is a cloud in it, and it is also the only thing \
	that limits how far along the layer a shallow ray can see towards the \
	horizon. Lower it first if the layer costs too much; raise it if the distant \
	clouds end abruptly.

option.DEBUG=Debug view
option.DEBUG.comment=Replaces the image with one of the buffers the pack works \
	in, which is how to find out what it thinks is happening when something looks \
	wrong.\n\n\
	None of these are meant to be played with; they exist for working out why a \
	scene looks the way it does.

option.FREEZE_ANIMATION_TIMER=Freeze animation timer
option.FREEZE_ANIMATION_TIMER.comment=Stops time for everything the frame timer \
	drives - water waves, waving foliage, clouds - so that a single frame can be \
	looked at closely without anything moving under it.\n\n\
	Everything else keeps running: this freezes the animations, not the game.

option.PREFER_FDM_GRADIENT=Prefer gradient fog
option.PREFER_FDM_GRADIENT.comment=Works out how much fog is in front of a water \
	surface with a coarser and cheaper method.\n\n\
	It is an approximation of an approximation, kept because the accurate path \
	needs derivatives that are not available in every situation. Leave it off \
	unless the accurate path is what is giving you trouble.

